{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And strip Aaron of his garments.</b> It says ״et <b>Aaron</b> et <b>his garments</b>״ — which makes it appear as though there are two strippings. Although this is not an elegant locution, it is nevertheless possible that the intent was to tell him that the garments he would strip off would be the priestly garments, not his personal garments. Therefore it says <b>strip Aaron</b> — of his very person — and then it goes back and stipulates that the stripping is of <b>his garments</b>, the ones designated for Aaron, which are the priestly garments, and concerning these it says <b>and you shall clothe Elazar with them</b>. This would not have been conveyed had it said ״and strip Aaron of his garments,״ for then there would be no superfluous word from which to derive a teaching, but only the straightforward meaning — as though it had said ״and strip Aaron from his garments.״ And the phrase <b>et his garments</b> is governed by ״strip״; whereas in its saying <b>strip et Aaron</b>, the phrase <b>et Aaron</b> is governed by ״strip,״ leaving the phrase <b>et his garments</b> superfluous for midrashic exposition." "<b>Further,</b> it may be intended to say along these lines: ״strip Aaron״ — and this stripping is only of his garments, which is what it means by <b>his garments</b>. But he has another garment immediately and at once, and this is the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni here), and this is their language: Aaron would strip and be clothed in clouds of glory. And from now on Aaron was never left without a garment, even at the time Moses was stripping him — he was stripped only of his garments, and the cloud would clothe each and every part of his body as it was being stripped from him."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:35.124514196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}